blocks:
  - type: metin
    baslik: The reality of studying abroad in North Cyprus
    metin: |-
      Famagusta is home to several universities, including Near East University's affiliated campuses, attracting students from over 100 countries. While the Mediterranean climate and multicultural environment create a vibrant student life, they also bring a set of psychological challenges that many students are unprepared for.

      Unlike domestic students who can return home for a weekend, international students often go months without seeing family or close friends. Add to this a new language, unfamiliar academic expectations, financial pressure and cultural differences — and the foundations for a mental health crisis are in place.
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: yesil
    baslik: By the numbers
    metin: Research consistently shows that international students experience higher rates of [depression](/en/blog/what-is-depression.html), [anxiety](/en/blog/what-is-anxiety-disorder.html) and loneliness than their domestic peers. A 2023 meta-analysis found that approximately 1 in 3 international students meets the clinical threshold for an anxiety or depressive disorder during their studies.
  - type: kart-yigini
    baslik: Common mental health struggles for international students
    kartlar:
      - baslik: Culture shock and identity confusion
        metin: Moving between cultures can destabilise your sense of identity. You may feel "too foreign" in Famagusta and "too changed" when you go home. This in-between experience is common — and it passes — but it can feel very disorienting in the moment.
      - baslik: Loneliness and homesickness
        metin: Homesickness is not "weakness" — it is a natural grief response to meaningful separation. Many students feel pressure to appear fine in front of new peers while struggling privately. Social media can make it worse, offering a curated highlight reel of friends' lives back home.
      - baslik: Academic pressure and performance anxiety
        metin: International students often carry enormous expectations — from families, sponsors and themselves. Studying in a second or third language intensifies this pressure. A single poor grade can feel catastrophic when financial and visa status depend on academic standing.
      - baslik: Financial stress and precarity
        metin: Currency fluctuations, limited work permits and dependence on remittances create a backdrop of financial anxiety that many students manage silently. This chronic low-grade stress has significant mental health consequences over time.
  - type: onayli-liste
    baslik: What professional support looks like
    giris: Talking to a psychologist is not a sign that something is "seriously wrong." It is a proactive investment in your wellbeing — the same way going to the gym is an investment in your physical health.
    maddeler:
      - baslik: A safe, confidential space
        aciklama: Everything you share stays between you and your psychologist. There is no judgement, no reporting to your university, no consequences for your academic status.
      - baslik: Practical coping strategies
        aciklama: You will not spend sessions just venting. Evidence-based therapies like CBT equip you with tools you can use immediately — for [exam stress](/en/blog/coping-with-exam-anxiety.html), low mood, relationship conflict and more.
      - baslik: Support without time pressure
        aciklama: Unlike a brief drop-in at a campus counselling office, regular therapy sessions allow you to work through issues in depth, at your own pace.
